    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p52-q77a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that the sum of the lengths of $OM$ and $ON$ is independent of the position of $P$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$OM+ON=\\sqrt p+(1-\\sqrt p)=1$. Find the tangent at $x=p$ and read its axis intercepts.",
          "worked": "For $0<p<1$, write $P=(p,(1-\\sqrt p)^2)$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(1-\\sqrt x)^2=1-2\\sqrt x+x\\\\\\frac{dy}{dx}&=1-\\frac1{\\sqrt x}\\\\m_P&=1-\\frac1{\\sqrt p}\\\\y-(1-\\sqrt p)^2&=\\left(1-\\frac1{\\sqrt p}\\right)(x-p)\\\\y&=\\left(1-\\frac1{\\sqrt p}\\right)x+1-\\sqrt p.\\end{align*}$$\nThe tangent meets the axes at $M=(\\sqrt p,0)$ and $N=(0,1-\\sqrt p)$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}OM+ON&=\\sqrt p+1-\\sqrt p\\\\OM+ON&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe sum is independent of $P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 88
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q77b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Is the area of $\\triangle OMN$ also independent of $P$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No. $\\text{Area}=\\tfrac12\\sqrt p(1-\\sqrt p)$, which varies with $p$.",
          "worked": "Using the intercepts from part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac12(OM)(ON)\\\\A&=\\frac12\\sqrt p(1-\\sqrt p).\\end{align*}$$\nFor example, $p=\\tfrac14$ gives $A=\\tfrac18$, while $p=\\tfrac19$ gives $A=\\tfrac19$. The area depends on $P$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
